Luis Siles (siles@medcor.mcgill.ca) wrote:

: I have located a post of someone that was looking for people to write a client
: for VMS but when I contacted him he said that nothing had come about and that
: he had the project on hold.

I looked (briefly) into what is needed to get an amanda client to run
under VMS. For those VMS users I'll briefly describe what the client program
has to do:

1) Listen on a nominated IP port for a UDP datagram. On receipt, provide
   a datagram indicating the amount of data that will be backed up for the
   given filesystem. An estimate of space required for an incremental backup
   is also needed.

2) At a later stage on receipt of another datagram, perform a backup (either
   a full or incremental) piping the output to a TCP socket on the remote
   system.

In both cases, it's just a simple matter of programming :-) Part 1) is
trivial. Part 2) is harder if you want to use VMS backup as the backup
program. I can't think of an elegant way of directing the output from 
backup to a TCP stream - hence my posting. Anyone got any suggestions?

I have thought of:

a) using DECnet to direct the output to another program (puke :-)

b) Creating a new device (AM:?) which looks like a tape but sends the data
   out as a TCP stream.

a) is easy, but b) requires programming (and a test system that you can
crash often :-)

Of course, for this to work you need to be running TCP/IP on your VMS system
but I take that as given.
